          %p such that
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                for each
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_f
                are the components of a (strict)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                          %mo ,
                          %mi B
                      %mo :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mi A
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo *
                      %mo ∘
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                          %mo ,
                          %mi B
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mo ˙
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mi B
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo *
                      %mo ∘
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                          %mo ,
                          %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\alpha_{A,B}:
                      (\alpha_A)^* \circ G_{A,B} \dot{\to} (\alpha_B)_* \circ
                      F_{A,B}
            %li
              %p
                the assignment
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ↦
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\mapsto \alpha_f
                behaves sensibly with respect to identities and composition (see the references for details).
          %p
            An
            %strong oplax natural transformation
            is as above, only with the
            = succeed "-cells" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_f
            reversed. This distinction is not entirely consistent in the literature; see the discussion of terminology below.
          %p
            An (op)lax natural transformation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            is a
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudonatural+transformation"} pseudonatural transformation
            if each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_f
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} invertible
            %strong strict natural transformation
            or
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-natural+transformation"} strict 2-natural transformation
            if each is an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+morphism"} identity
          %p In all of these cases, the word ‘natural’ is often dropped for brevity.
          %h2#categories_and_categories_of_lax_transformations
            Categories and
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            Lax transformations from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            and
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modification"} modification
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Lax
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo ,
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lax(F,G)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ps
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ps(F,G)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Oplax
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Oplax(F,G)
            consisting of pseudo-natural and oplax transformations, respectively.
          %p
            Pushing it up a notch, for 2-categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            we have hom-2-categories
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %msub
                      %mi Cat
                      %mi lax
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2Cat_{lax}(C,D)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %msub
                      %mi Cat
                      %mi ps
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2Cat_{ps}(C,D)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi Cat
                    %mi oplax
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2Cat_{oplax}(C,D)
            whose objects are 2-functors (generally taken to be strong or strict), whose morphisms are lax, pseudo, or oplax transformations respectively, and whose 2-cells are modifications. These hom-2-categories are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+homs"} internal homs
            for the various version of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gray+tensor+product"} Gray tensor product
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2Cat
          %p
            Finally, there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-category"} 3-category
            consisting of 2-categories, (strong or strict) 2-functors, pseudo-natural transformations, and modifications. No laxness is possible at this level (without “laxifying” the notion of 3-category).
          %h2#lax_versus_oplax “Lax” versus “oplax”
          %p
            The choice of which direction to call “lax” and which to call “oplax” is not made consistently in the literature. The convention used above is Benabou’s original choice, as well as that of Leinster, Borceaux, and the majority of Australian writing on category theory. However, some references, such as the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elephant"} Elephant
          %p
            It is arguably the case that the direction we call “oplax” occurs more commonly in practice. For instance,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/icons"} icons
            are a special sort of oplax transformations (although if “lax” and “oplax” were switched, then the acronymic derivation of the word “icon” would no longer work). Likewise, when monoidal categories are viewed as one-object 2-categories,
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+natural+transformation"} monoidal natural transformation
          %p
            On the other hand, the convention used above (besides having a little more weight of tradition) has the advantage that there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-monad"} 2-monad
            whose algebras are 2-functors, and for which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+morphism"} lax and oplax algebra morphisms
            are precisely lax and oplax transformations, respectively, under this convention. Thus, for instance, theorems such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/doctrinal+adjunction"} doctrinal adjunction
            can be applied to lax and oplax transformations without needing to switch back and forth between two different meanings of “lax.”
          %h2#the_yoneda_lemma The Yoneda Lemma
          %p
            Given a bicategory
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F:C^{op}\to Cat
            and a
            = succeed "-cell" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\in C
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjoint functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo :
                  %mi Lax
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I : Lax(y A,F) \stackrel{\to}{\leftarrow} F(A) : J
          %p
            such that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J \dashv I
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi Cat
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y A:C \to Cat^{C^{op}}
            is the image of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            under the bicategorical
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
          .proof
            %h6#proof_sketch Proof (sketch)
            %p
              For
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\in F(A)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi y
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J(a):y A\Rightarrow F
              be the lax transformation defined by
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi J
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi F
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J(a)_B(f) = (F f)(a)
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi J
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ˙
                    %mi J
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi Y
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J(a)_g : F(g) \circ J(a)_X \dot{\to} J(a)_Y \circ g_*
            %p
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g:X\to Y
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              are given by
              = succeed "\u2018s" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mrow
                          %mi g
                          %mo ,
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (F_{g,-})_a
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              wrt the associator and left unitor of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi a
                    %mo →
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:a\to b
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(A)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J(x)
              is a modification because
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo ,
                        %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{g,f}
              is a natural transformation (i.e. 2-cell in
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I(\alpha) = \alpha_A(1_A)
              as usual. For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo :
                    %mi α
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ¨
                    %mi β
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m:\alpha\ddot{\to}\beta
              a modification,
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi I
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi m
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi m
                        %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I(m)=(m_A)(1_A)
            %p
              The unit
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                    %mo :
                    %mi F
                    %mi A
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ˙
                    %mi I
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta: F A \dot{\to} I J
              at
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi F
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\in F A
              is the component
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (F_A)_a
              of
              = succeed "\u2018s" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              by definition. The counit
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon_\alpha
              is obtained via the usual 1-chasing, and is given in components by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mi α
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi r
                      %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\epsilon_\alpha)_B(f) = \alpha_B(r_f) \circ (\alpha_f)(1_A)
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
              is the right unitor of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mo :
                      %mi J
                      %mi I
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mo ˙
                      %mi Lax
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon: J I \dot{\to} Lax(y A,F)
            %p
              The triangle identities may be proved by expanding the definitions above and using the coherence conditions on lax functors and transformations and coherence of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %p
              See Gray for the case
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-categories"} strict 2-categories
              and strict 2-functors.
